    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"How Closely the Models Match Bree’s Coding\"\nauthor: \"Manuel Alcalá Kovalski, Urban Institute\"\ndate: today\ntoc-depth: 2\nexecute:\n  warning: false\n  message: false\n---\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBree coded the canvassing and survey files in her September sample in NVivo\nwith codebook version 5, 71 open-ended answers in all. We compared\nher codes with the codes Sonnet and Opus gave the same answers, coding with the\nsame codebook. The\n[last section](#memo) has a short write-up of the methods and results for the\nqualitative researcher memo.\n\n64 of the 71 answers respond\nto one canvassing question: **“What's one thing you would change about care, whether it's for your kids, aging family members, people with disabilities, yourself, or the people around you? (Optional)”**\nThe other 7 are survey answers.\n\n## Sonnet matches Bree as well as Opus does\n\n**Sonnet agrees with Bree as closely as Opus does and costs less than half as\nmuch per answer ($0.0047 versus $0.0101), so Sonnet is probably the way to go.**\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n# share of codes in common: both empty counts as identical\njaccard <- function(a, b) {\n  either <- union(a, b)\n  if (length(either) == 0) return(1)\n  length(intersect(a, b)) / length(either)\n}\n\n# MASI distance (Passonneau 2006): 0 = identical sets, 1 = nothing in common.\n# Shared share of codes, weighted by how the sets overlap: identical 1, one\n# contains the other 2/3, partial overlap 1/3, none 0\nmasi_distance <- function(a, b) {\n  if (length(union(a, b)) == 0) return(0)\n  shared <- length(intersect(a, b)) / length(union(a, b))\n  weight <- if (setequal(a, b)) 1\n    else if (all(a %in% b) || all(b %in% a)) 2 / 3\n    else if (length(intersect(a, b)) > 0) 1 / 3\n    else 0\n  1 - shared * weight\n}\n\n# distance between every pair of code sets, filled once so the bootstrap only\n# indexes into it\ndistance_matrix <- function(sets, distance) {\n  n <- length(sets)\n  d <- matrix(0, n, n)\n  for (i in seq_len(n - 1)) {\n    for (j in (i + 1):n) d[i, j] <- d[j, i] <- distance(sets[[i]], sets[[j]])\n  }\n  d\n}\n\n# Krippendorff's alpha for two coders: 1 - observed / expected disagreement.\n# `d` holds the first coder's sets, then the second's, so answer i is row i\n# and row n + i. Observed: the two coders on the same answer. Expected: any\n# two sets drawn from all the sets both coders produced.\nalpha_from_distances <- function(d, idx) {\n  n <- nrow(d) / 2\n  pooled <- c(idx, n + idx)\n  observed <- mean(d[cbind(idx, n + idx)])\n  m <- length(pooled)\n  expected <- sum(d[pooled, pooled]) / (m * (m - 1))\n  1 - observed / expected\n}\n\n# the three measures for two coders, each with a 95 percent bootstrap interval\npair_agreement <- function(x, y) {\n  both <- lengths(map2(x, y, intersect))\n  d <- distance_matrix(c(x, y), masi_distance)\n  measures <- function(idx) {\n    c(\n      found  = sum(both[idx]) / sum(lengths(x)[idx]),\n      agreed = sum(both[idx]) / sum(lengths(y)[idx]),\n      alpha  = alpha_from_distances(d, idx)\n    )\n  }\n  estimate <- measures(seq_along(x))\n  boot <- replicate(n_boot, measures(sample(length(x), replace = TRUE)))\n  tibble(\n    measure = names(estimate),\n    estimate,\n    low  = apply(boot, 1, quantile, 0.025),\n    high = apply(boot, 1, quantile, 0.975)\n  )\n}\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nshow <- function(r, what) {\n  r <- filter(r, measure == what)\n  if (what == \"alpha\") sprintf(\"%.2f (%.2f–%.2f)\", r$estimate, r$low, r$high)\n  else sprintf(\"%.0f%% (%.0f–%.0f)\", 100 * r$estimate, 100 * r$low, 100 * r$high)\n}\n\nall_codes <- map(names(runs), \\(m) pair_agreement(code_sets$Bree, code_sets[[m]]))\ntopics    <- map(names(runs), \\(m) pair_agreement(code_sets$Bree, same_topics[[m]]))\nmodels    <- pair_agreement(code_sets$Sonnet, code_sets$Opus)\n\nsummary_table <- tibble(\n  Measure = c(\n    \"Bree’s codes the model also found\",\n    \"Model’s codes Bree also used\",\n    \"Model’s codes Bree also used, on topics Bree coded\",\n    \"Krippendorff’s alpha\",\n    \"Krippendorff’s alpha, on topics Bree coded\"\n  ),\n  `Bree and Sonnet` = c(show(all_codes[[1]], \"found\"), show(all_codes[[1]], \"agreed\"),\n                        show(topics[[1]], \"agreed\"), show(all_codes[[1]], \"alpha\"),\n                        show(topics[[1]], \"alpha\")),\n  `Bree and Opus`   = c(show(all_codes[[2]], \"found\"), show(all_codes[[2]], \"agreed\"),\n                        show(topics[[2]], \"agreed\"), show(all_codes[[2]], \"alpha\"),\n                        show(topics[[2]], \"alpha\")),\n  `Sonnet and Opus` = c(\"\", \"\", \"\", show(models, \"alpha\"), \"\")\n)\n\nsummary_table |>\n  tbl(note = \"95 percent intervals in brackets, from resampling the answers 2,000 times.\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"reactable html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-aa4caf59def89ce39c53\" style=\"width:auto;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-aa4caf59def89ce39c53\">{\"x\":{\"tag\":{\"name\":\"Reactable\",\"attribs\":{\"data\":{\"Measure\":[\"Bree’s codes the model also found\",\"Model’s codes Bree also used\",\"Model’s codes Bree also used, on topics Bree coded\",\"Krippendorff’s alpha\",\"Krippendorff’s alpha, on topics Bree coded\"],\"Bree and Sonnet\":[\"49% (39–59)\",\"35% (28–44)\",\"73% (64–81)\",\"0.29 (0.20–0.37)\",\"0.38 (0.27–0.48)\"],\"Bree and Opus\":[\"54% (45–64)\",\"35% (28–43)\",\"71% (62–80)\",\"0.29 (0.20–0.37)\",\"0.37 (0.26–0.47)\"],\"Sonnet and Opus\":[\"\",\"\",\"\",\"0.53 (0.43–0.61)\",\"\"]},\"columns\":[{\"id\":\"Measure\",\"name\":\"Measure\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":90},{\"id\":\"Bree and Sonnet\",\"name\":\"Bree and Sonnet\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":90},{\"id\":\"Bree and Opus\",\"name\":\"Bree and Opus\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":90},{\"id\":\"Sonnet and Opus\",\"name\":\"Sonnet and Opus\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":90}],\"resizable\":true,\"pagination\":false,\"defaultPageSize\":60,\"showPageSizeOptions\":false,\"striped\":true,\"style\":{\"fontSize\":\"14px\"},\"theme\":{\"borderColor\":\"#e5e7eb\",\"cellPadding\":\"8px 12px\",\"headerStyle\":{\"background\":\"#f3f4f6\",\"fontWeight\":600}},\"dataKey\":\"6001fe37d2110bc7eb4dd4012bd20f39\"},\"children\":[]},\"class\":\"reactR_markup\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n<p class=\"table-note\">95 percent intervals in brackets, from resampling the answers 2,000 times.</p>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n\n- Each model finds about half of Bree’s codes, and alpha is\n  0.29 for\n  both. On Bree’s scale, that is fair agreement, well below the substantial\n  agreement (0.7 to 0.8) we were hoping for. Most of the difference comes down\n  to the two issues in the next section.\n- The two models agree with each other about twice as much (alpha\n  0.53, moderate\n  agreement). The gap\n  with Bree comes from how the task is set up, not from which model does it.\n\n## What drives the disagreement\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n**1. The models code every topic an answer mentions; Bree appears to code only\nthe answer to the question.** The models use 2.4\nto 2.6 codes per answer, compared\nwith 1.7 for Bree. Most of the\nextras are on topics Bree did not code on that answer, such as the hardship\nsomeone describes when asked what they would change. Leaving those out, about\n7 in 10 model codes match hers. This explains about half the gap, and **Bree’s\nanswer on which rule she follows tells us which rule to give the models.**\n\n**2. Two codes account for about half of what the models miss.** Of the codes\nBree chose that a model did not, 51% to\n55% are `NEED_HIGH_QUALITY_CARE` or\n`NEED_OTHER`. Bree uses `NEED_HIGH_QUALITY_CARE` for answers about caregiver\npay, where the models choose `SOLUTION_CAREGIVER_WAGES`, and `NEED_OTHER` where\nthe models find a specific code. **Where the line falls between these codes is a\nquestion for Bree.**\n\nThree answers that show each pattern (codes a model shares with Bree in bold):\n\n\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell .column-page}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nall_agree <- which(\n  map2_lgl(code_sets$Bree, code_sets$Sonnet, setequal) &\n    map2_lgl(code_sets$Bree, code_sets$Opus, setequal) &\n    map_lgl(code_sets$Bree, \\(s) any(c(\"NEED_CHILD_CARE\", \"NEED_SENIOR_CARE\") %in% s))\n)[1]\n\nexamples <- c(\n  which(str_starts(scope$text, \"Pay workers more\")),  # different pay code + extra topic\n  which(str_to_lower(str_trim(scope$text)) == \"money\"), # NEED_OTHER versus a specific code\n  all_agree                                            # all three agree\n)\nif (length(examples) != 3) cli::cli_abort(\"An example answer was not found\")\n\nside_by_side[examples, ] |>\n  select(-Question) |>\n  tbl(columns = code_columns[-1])\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"reactable html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-1cf19b118c61304e7645\" style=\"width:auto;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-1cf19b118c61304e7645\">{\"x\":{\"tag\":{\"name\":\"Reactable\",\"attribs\":{\"data\":{\"Answer\":[\"Pay workers more it's a big burnout career not enough support quality can't be as good if we are burned out\",\"Money\",\"free child care\"],\"Bree\":[\"NEED_<wbr>HIGH_<wbr>QUALITY_<wbr>CARE<br>NEED_<wbr>SUPPORT_<wbr>CAREGIVERS\",\"NEED_<wbr>OTHER\",\"NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<br>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE\"],\"Sonnet\":[\"CHALLENGE_<wbr>EMOTIONAL_<wbr>STRESS<br>CHALLENGE_<wbr>QUALITY_<wbr>CONCERNS<br><b>NEED_<wbr>SUPPORT_<wbr>CAREGIVERS<\\/b><br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WAGES\",\"NEED_<wbr>DIRECT_<wbr>FINANCIAL_<wbr>ASSISTANCE\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<\\/b>\"],\"Opus\":[\"CHALLENGE_<wbr>EMOTIONAL_<wbr>STRESS<br>CHALLENGE_<wbr>QUALITY_<wbr>CONCERNS<br>PAID_<wbr>CARE_<wbr>WORKER<br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WAGES\",\"(none)\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<\\/b>\"]},\"columns\":[{\"id\":\"Answer\",\"name\":\"Answer\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":240},{\"id\":\"Bree\",\"name\":\"Bree\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true},{\"id\":\"Sonnet\",\"name\":\"Sonnet\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true},{\"id\":\"Opus\",\"name\":\"Opus\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true}],\"resizable\":true,\"pagination\":false,\"defaultPageSize\":60,\"showPageSizeOptions\":false,\"striped\":true,\"style\":{\"fontSize\":\"14px\"},\"theme\":{\"borderColor\":\"#e5e7eb\",\"cellPadding\":\"8px 12px\",\"headerStyle\":{\"background\":\"#f3f4f6\",\"fontWeight\":600}},\"dataKey\":\"882893f9b99861a91135fc5436c59e3a\"},\"children\":[]},\"class\":\"reactR_markup\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n\n## How to improve the model coding\n\n- Once Bree answers the first question below, add her rule to the coding\n  prompt and rerun these answers. This costs under a dollar.\n- Add decision rules to the codebook for the caregiver-pay codes and for when\n  `NEED_OTHER` applies. The prompt reads the codebook, so the models pick these\n  up on the next run.\n\n## Questions for Bree\n\n1. **Should every topic an answer mentions get a code, or only the answer to\n   the question asked?** When someone answering “what would you change” also\n   describes a hardship, the models code the hardship and Bree does not. This\n   explains about half the gap, and her answer tells us which rule to give the\n   models.\n2. **Where is the line between `NEED_HIGH_QUALITY_CARE` and the caregiver-pay\n   codes, and when does `NEED_OTHER` apply?** These two codes are about half of\n   what the models miss.\n\n## All 71 answers side by side\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\" appearance=\"minimal\"}\n## Show the table\n\nCodes a model shares with Bree are in bold. [Type in the search box to find an\nanswer or a code.]{.web-only}\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell .column-page}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nside_by_side |>\n  tbl(page_size = 10, searchable = TRUE, columns = code_columns)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"reactable html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-b967a3a576e2639af0cb\" style=\"width:auto;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-b967a3a576e2639af0cb\">{\"x\":{\"tag\":{\"name\":\"Reactable\",\"attribs\":{\"data\":{\"Question\":[\"Survey q5a\",\"Survey q5a\",\"Survey q5a\",\"Survey q5c\",\"Survey q7\",\"Survey q7\",\"Survey q7\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\",\"Canvass Q3\"],\"Answer\":[\"Working with key stakeholders in creating strategic partnerships to bridge gaps for community folx\",\"I am now dealing with my 4th person with dementia (3 family members and 1 friend). The help has come from paid caregivers, family members, friends, and healthcare professionals. For my friend, he had dementia as a result of his Parkinson's and I was his healthcare representative (going with him to appointments, advocating for him, and managing his health needs. It took 5 of us to take care of him. I don't know how i would have managed without the other 4 people. My mom had Alzheimer's, and our family benefitted greatly from the support services provided by the [REDACTED DEMENTIA ORGANIZATION].\",\"They assist the parents in my Center.\",\"The government helps with vouchers.\",\"Life beyond work and care. Time to have a hobby again. Space for creative expression.\",\"I believe we would have safer, flourishing communities if we were all taken care of (paid leave, universal child care, liveable wages) etc\",\"I would be less afraid of aging, less worried about how to care for family\",\"Money\",\"More access more info and education for caregivers it feels like an abyss when you are in it\",\"More accessible\",\"Make it free universal cradle to grave free for all\",\"the first thing that comes to mind for me is like a maternity leave and things like that because I mean, I know in the United States very good especially so I feel like I would definitely want to change that adding parental leave in general because I know for some of the caregivers in my life, they have not been able to I would really like to see that.\",\"Think it needs to be built in is more of a system and a network versus an afterthought like proactive versus reactive when people are already like past the point of needing help\",\"Early childhood care and education, a so please come affordable for all thank you we got it.\",\"What would you change? I think like after school programs and supplemental care like outside of school hours I would like really life-changing for a lot of people in my life.\",\"free child care\",\"Hope to make child care universal and accessible to all\",\"Making info more available about services\",\"Having ways to give people ways to know about what's\",\"Eduction and access to educate\",\"More access to care parental leave\",\"More access to healthcare and insurance and time of day\",\"Way more affordable but people doing it deserve to be paid well\",\"More affordability\",\"More access able\",\"More access[i]ble\",\"Peaceful and not too hard in the worker or the elderly\",\"More equality for everyone making sure everyone is taken care of\",\"Subsidized care better for all so care takers and people being cared for have good quality of life\",\"More for elderly accessibility more non traditional opportunities for extended family to come support flexible at work to care for family ||My cousin was in hospice luckily my employer let me go be with her\",\"Wish more facilities to house our elderly and how to navigate on the reservation rather than having to go outside the reservation transport big problem and we want them in the community with us\",\"For child care my sister has it so hard to find child care everything is so expensive for even a week so expensive diabolical how inaccessible it is e dry thing is double standards we want them at home but they can't afford it you want to work you need a nanny you have to pay it's all too much\",\"In nyc about to have our first child maybe process of finding child care I don't know how to navigate the system how can I be more supportive of child care providers subsidies for child care\",\"What do you do? Yeah, right right better resources for learning about quality elder care\",\"More support for caregivers they are giving up wages social opportunities career opportunities\",\"More affordable accessibility\",\"Give protections health care safety so many things\",\"This country needs to reform elder care how do nursing homes not have enough staffing\",\"Everything child care sucks elder care terrible seeing loss of funding in programs for kids in New Mexico they gave free child care but it's crazy some states get that some don't imbalance is crazy they keep taking more we have a deficit of care\",\"Caregiver insurance to nanny, benefits, PTO benefits i had no protection as a nanny\",\"Better training more accessible for caregivers also mental health\",\"Good training having access to care sometimes people not allowed access to care\",\"Accessible support for caregivers\",\"More resources that aren't policed workplace abuses aren't reported because of policing\",\"Less expensive more accessible some people can't afford it it can happen so quick that you need it\",\"Support system government funds to help\",\"I live with my grandma in my career trying to be a nurse it takes a lot to give to others self care so important there's a lot of efforts to talk about it but we need more unity social groups motivating to care for yourself understanding benefits of self care passion is imoirtant mental health is key restorative environment\",\"Encourage people to take up these jobs\",\"Publically available resources\",\"Taking away loopholes to prove you need it making it legitimate as. Career\",\"I'm taking care of my grandma it's hard in the whole family it's heartbreaking I think about people who don't have family to help||Affordable healthcare for seniors is needed it's so expensive lots of elders not even buying groceries healthcare should be free ||You face different levels of struggles trying to be strong for more than yourself ||Being positive while you are suffering ||Meeting people\",\"If we could get more funding to provide free care whether parents or elderly it's so expensive and hard when families have to choose you can't always do both quality care and time\",\"My grandma was dying and I helped with her care and was close to her meaningful to be a caregiver and help her then I got chronic disease it's easier to give than accept for me ||Caregivers need more support caretakers are backbone they need more support I'm lucky we cited help my grandmas caregivers we become so close ||Medical overhaul believing women's pain ||Emotions discredited ||Affordable child care I a, inspired by nyc child care takes a village\",\"More resources to make care more accessible more background checks\",\"Free care for all in