",\"CARE_<wbr>VALUED<br><b>NEED_<wbr>ACCESS_<wbr>GENERAL<\\/b>\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br>SOLUTION_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>SUPPORT_<wbr>CAREGIVERS<\\/b><br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WAGES\",\"COMMUNITY_<wbr>RESPONSIBILITY<br>FAIRNESS_<wbr>AND_<wbr>EQUITY<br>SOLUTION_<wbr>GOVERNMENT_<wbr>PAID\",\"NEED_<wbr>ACCESS_<wbr>GENERAL<br><b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WAGES\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>SUPPORT_<wbr>CAREGIVERS<\\/b><br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WORKFORCE_<wbr>DEV\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>PARENTAL_<wbr>LEAVE<\\/b><br>NEED_<wbr>SUPPORT_<wbr>CAREGIVERS\",\"CARE_<wbr>SETTING_<wbr>CENTER<br>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<br>FAIRNESS_<wbr>AND_<wbr>EQUITY<br>NEED_<wbr>HIGH_<wbr>QUALITY_<wbr>CARE<br>PAID_<wbr>CARE_<wbr>WORKER\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b>\",\"IDEAL_<wbr>SYSTEM_<wbr>CARE_<wbr>DIGNITY_<wbr>RESPECT\",\"<b>NEED_<wbr>AFFORDABLE_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>CHILD_<wbr>CARE<\\/b><br><b>NEED_<wbr>HEALTH_<wbr>CARE<\\/b>\",\"CHALLENGE_<wbr>EMOTIONAL_<wbr>STRESS<br>CHALLENGE_<wbr>QUALITY_<wbr>CONCERNS<br>PAID_<wbr>CARE_<wbr>WORKER<br>SOLUTION_<wbr>CAREGIVER_<wbr>WAGES\"]},\"columns\":[{\"id\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Question\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":80},{\"id\":\"Answer\",\"name\":\"Answer\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":240},{\"id\":\"Bree\",\"name\":\"Bree\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true},{\"id\":\"Sonnet\",\"name\":\"Sonnet\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true},{\"id\":\"Opus\",\"name\":\"Opus\",\"type\":\"character\",\"minWidth\":170,\"html\":true}],\"resizable\":true,\"searchable\":true,\"defaultPageSize\":10,\"showPageSizeOptions\":false,\"striped\":true,\"style\":{\"fontSize\":\"14px\"},\"theme\":{\"borderColor\":\"#e5e7eb\",\"cellPadding\":\"8px 12px\",\"headerStyle\":{\"background\":\"#f3f4f6\",\"fontWeight\":600}},\"dataKey\":\"9d46fb7349ed1e42bf5c20feb428c6bf\"},\"children\":[]},\"class\":\"reactR_markup\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n:::\n\n## Text for the memo {#memo}\n\nTo copy into the qualitative researcher memo and edit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTo check how closely the NLP coding matches human coding, Manu compared Bree’s\nNVivo coding of the September 22 random selection, 71 open-ended\nanswers (64 from the canvass export and\n7 from the open-ended survey export), with\nthe coding of the same answers by two large language models, Claude Sonnet 5\nand Claude Opus 5.5. The models were given the same codebook (version 5,\n145 codes) and written coding rules and did not see Bree’s\ncodes.\nNVivo exports do not include respondent IDs, so coded passages were matched to\nanswers by text. Because an answer can carry several codes, agreement was\nmeasured on each answer’s full set of codes with Krippendorff’s alpha using the\nMASI distance (Passonneau, 2006), with 95 percent confidence intervals from\nresampling the answers.\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|Coders          |Krippendorff’s alpha |Share of Bree’s codes found |\n|:---------------|:--------------------|:---------------------------|\n|Bree and Sonnet |0.29 (0.20–0.37)     |49%                         |\n|Bree and Opus   |0.29 (0.20–0.37)     |54%                         |\n|Sonnet and Opus |0.53 (0.43–0.61)     |                            |\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n\nAgreement between Bree and each model was fair (alpha = 0.29; Landis\n& Koch, 1977), below the 0.75 threshold. The two models agreed with each other\nat a moderate level (alpha = 0.53), and Sonnet matched Bree as\nclosely as Opus at less than half the cost. Two patterns explain most of the\ngap. The models coded every topic an answer mentioned\n(2.4 to\n2.6 codes per answer, compared with\n1.7 for Bree), whereas Bree appears\nto code only the content that answers the question; limited to the topics Bree\ncoded, alpha rises to 0.38. And about half the codes the models\nmissed were NEED_HIGH_QUALITY_CARE, where the models chose\nSOLUTION_CAREGIVER_WAGES, or NEED_OTHER, where they chose a more specific code.\nNext, Bree will set rules for both, Manu will add them to the coding prompt and\ncodebook, and the models will recode the same 71 answers.\n",
